  "worked_solution": "The graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-5,-1)$ and the line segment for $x\\in (-1,2].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,= & {\\int }_{-5}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+{\\int }_{-1}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{-1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {\\int }_{-5}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,= & \\,{{{\\int }_{-5}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{\u23df}}_{{A}_{1}}\\,+\\,{{{\\int }_{-1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{\u23df}}_{{A}_{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-5,-1).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-1,2].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =7.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\, & =\\,{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\,6+7.5 \\\\  & =\\,13.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
